      id: "explanation-26", type: "explanation", variant: "simple",
      content: "A cooperative is an autonomous association of persons who join voluntarily to meet common economic, social and cultural needs through a jointly owned and democratically controlled enterprise. Its philosophy is 'Each for all and all for each'. Unlike a company, where votes follow shares, a cooperative runs on 'One Member, One Vote' — so the smallest farmer counts as much as the largest.\n\nWhy does India need them? Small-holding farmers and fragmented producers have almost no individual bargaining power. Cooperatives supply the economies of scale needed to bargain with large corporations — cheap credit through PACS, market linkages that cut out middlemen, and a share of the final price for the producer rather than the trader.\n\nThe legal architecture is split. Cooperation sits in the State List (Entry 32), so state legislatures make the law for societies operating within a single state, supervised by the Registrar of Cooperative Societies (RCS). But Multi-State Cooperative Societies (MSCS) — those operating in more than one state — fall in the Union List (Entry 44), governed by Parliament through the MSCS Act, 2002 (amended 2022, which created the Co-operative Election Authority).\n\nThe 97th Amendment tried to impose a uniform democratic template on all cooperatives — fixed board size, reservations, five-year terms, compulsory audits, capped supersession. The catch: Part IXB regulated a State List subject, so under Art 368(2) it needed ratification by half the state legislatures. It never got it. In Union of India v. Rajendra N. Shah (2021), the Supreme Court struck down Part IXB for cooperatives within states, but severed and upheld it for MSCS. Article 19(1)(c) and Article 43B were untouched and remain fully in force.",
